---
/**
 * /products/pro — Polaris Pro, on the cinematic product layout.
 *
 * The page is the record in data/products/pro.ts plus the two blocks below,
 * which stay as markup on purpose:
 *
 *   spec-tables — the inline specification sheet. Every row carries its own
 *     citation and several carry [verify:] flags that initVerifyFlags and
 *     scripts/check-claims.py both look for. Turning it into data would mean
 *     retyping every ruled fact on the page, which CLAUDE.md forbids.
 *   upsell — the .g3band. Its argument is specific to this tier.
 */
import ProductShowcase from "../../layouts/ProductShowcase.astro";
import { PRO } from "../../data/products/pro";
---

<ProductShowcase {...PRO}>
  <Fragment slot="spec-tables">
      <details class="mtbl" open><summary><b>Sharing</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr><tr><td>Simultaneous shares in the workspace</td><td class="y">Up to 10 live sources composited side by side, laid out automatically as sources join and leave. Pro composites up to 10; Essentials runs the same software with a ceiling of 5, set by memory on the smaller chassis rather than by licensing [Ruled by Damian Blazy 12 Aug 2026 - see src/data/rulings.ts]</td></tr><tr><td>Web-join from the browser: no app, no download</td><td class="y">&#10003; share from the device you walked in with</td></tr><tr><td>Native AirPlay &middot; Miracast &middot; Google Cast (LAN)</td><td class="y">&#10003; all three, built in: cast from Apple, Windows and Chromebook devices on the room network with nothing installed [verify: BOTH released data sheets - Pro DS-MCS.PRO-05072025 and Essentials DS-MCS.ESS-02192025 - list protocols as WebRTC, AirPlay and Miracast only, with no Google Cast. Two published documents against one headline capability: engineering to confirm and the sheets to be reissued before launch]</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile sharing &middot; phones and tablets</td><td class="y">&#10003; the Polaris app for iOS and Android: share your screen, photos or a live whiteboard, and control the workspace from your seat</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Network</b><span class="mtbl-n">3 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr><tr><td>802.1X network authentication</td><td class="y">&#10003; EAP-TLS, PEAP, TTLS or EAP-PWD, on wired Ethernet and on Wi-Fi (WPA2-Enterprise). Supply a CA certificate, a client certificate and a private key; the supplied private key is held encrypted at rest under a key that is itself sealed in the NXP SE050 secure element, rather than in plaintext configuration [verify: approved by Damian Blazy 13 Aug 2026 from the verification document. Engineering to confirm the wrapping mechanism end to end, since the document's own evidence elsewhere describes the 802.1X password as a NetworkManager system secret - engineering and CISO]</td></tr><tr><td>Dual-network posture: corp + guest, isolated</td><td class="y">Wired GbE and the wireless radio run simultaneously, and the pod does not forward between them: its firewall drops all forwarded traffic and the firmware never enables IP forwarding. Corporate on the wire, guests on Wi-Fi &mdash; separated because the device is not a router, not because of a policy someone could unmake [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024: one wired port; posture is wired + wireless]</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-network relay: subnets / VLANs / internet</td><td class="y">&#10003; cloud-negotiated direct connections; signaling is outbound-only TLS on TCP 443; no inbound firewall rules</td></tr></table><p class="note" style="margin:8px 4px 2px">Plain language: 802.1x means the network itself demands the device prove who it is before granting a port; EAP-TLS proves it with a certificate instead of a password. Dual-network posture means the device sits on two networks at once, kept isolated, so guests share without ever touching the corporate LAN.</p></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Security</b><span class="mtbl-n">8 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr><tr><td>Encryption in transit</td><td class="y">Signalling and fleet management run outbound over HTTPS to Polaris Cloud. The device exposes no TLS listener of its own, so a port scan of the pod finds nothing to negotiate against &mdash; the encryption that matters is on the outbound leg, not on an open port in your room [verify: the verification document proposes publishing that the cloud negotiates TLS 1.3 or 1.2 and refuses 1.0 and 1.1. That is a minimum-version claim, which this site&rsquo;s hard rule forbids until the 7 July 2026 finding is retested. Re-measure, then decide whether to state versions here - engineering and CISO]</td></tr><tr><td>Secure boot &middot; secure element</td><td class="y">&#10003; secure boot is mandatory on production firmware; NXP SE050 secure element on board holds the device&rsquo;s own identity and update-verification keys in hardware</td></tr><tr><td>TAA-compliant supply chain</td><td class="y">&#10003; built with Advantech in Taiwan, a TAA-designated country, with the bare printed circuit board the only China-sourced item in the build [verify: confirm the PCB is the sole China-sourced item, and that the fabricator is named on the country-of-origin attestation - supply chain] [final claim wording with legal]</td></tr><tr><td>Linux-hardened OS</td><td class="y">&#10003; purpose-built, minimised Linux at the display: no Windows attack surface, no general-purpose desktop</td></tr><tr><td>Key custody: where private keys live</td><td class="y">&#10003; device private keys are generated and used <em>inside</em> the NXP SE050 secure element and never cross its boundary. The channel between the application and the secure element is itself authenticated and encrypted (GlobalPlatform SCP-03), and the keys for that channel are held in ARM TrustZone secure firmware where neither the operating system nor any application can read them. [Architecture per the Gen 4 Pod security requirements specification; see the <a href="/platform/security">Trust Center</a>]</td></tr><tr><td>Signed updates, no downgrade &mdash; and automatic rollback on a failed load</td><td class="y">&#10003; two different things, and both matter. Update packages are checked for authenticity and integrity before they are applied, and the device will not accept a <b>downgrade</b> to an earlier version, so a known-vulnerable firmware cannot be pushed back onto a Pod. Separately, the pod keeps <b>two firmware partitions</b>: a new image installs beside the running one rather than over it, and a pod that fails to come up on the new version comes back up on the last known-good one. Deliberate downgrade is refused; a bad load recovers itself. [Architecture per the Gen 4 Pod security requirements specification; rollback behaviour confirmed by Damian Blazy 14 Aug 2026]</td></tr><tr><td>Content that does not persist</td><td class="y">&#10003; shared content is composited on the fly rather than written to the device, and ephemeral session data &mdash; content, session keys and generated random values &mdash; is overwritten at the end of every session, including sessions that are abandoned rather than ended cleanly.</td></tr><tr><td>Physical: Kensington Nano Security Slot</td><td class="y">&#10003; on the chassis [data sheet]</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>I/O</b><span class="mtbl-n">7 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr><tr><td>HDMI output: dual display</td><td class="y">2&times; HDMI 2.0, dual 4K (3840 &times; 2160) at 60&nbsp;Hz; 1080p and 720p up to 60&nbsp;Hz [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]. CEC for display power and input control [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] [verify: the data sheet does not mention CEC - confirm for the published spec, hardware team]</td></tr><tr><td>HDMI input: wired ingest / room-system augmentation</td><td class="y">1&times; HDMI 2.0 in, with HDCP 2.0 content protection [data sheet]. A wired source takes the display over as a full-screen feed rather than joining the workspace canvas, so it stays in the room &mdash; remote seats receive the workspace, and a wired source is not part of it. Share the same device wirelessly instead and it lands in the workspace like anything else, remote seats included [Ruled by Damian Blazy 14 Aug 2026]</td></tr><tr><td>Ethernet</td><td class="y">1&times; 10/100/1000 RJ-45, PoE+ (802.3at Type 2) [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr><tr><td>Audio out</td><td class="y">&#10003; a dedicated analogue audio-out jack on the rear panel, so room audio can go straight to a soundbar or a DSP without passing through the display [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr><tr><td>Video codecs</td><td>H.264 encode and decode &middot; H.265 encode and decode &middot; VP8 decode &middot; AV1 decode &middot; JPEG/MJPEG decode &mdash; all required. VP9 decode is preferred rather than required. Hardware colour conversion: YUY2/YUYV [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi radio</td><td class="y">MediaTek MT7921: Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax), 802.11 a/b/g/n/ac/ax compliant, 2.4 + 5 GHz, 2&times;2 MIMO [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]; Bluetooth (MediaTek MT7921) [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] [verify: the data sheet states neither the bands nor Bluetooth - confirm both for the published spec, hardware team]</td></tr><tr><td>USB: peripherals (camera, mic, speaker, touch)</td><td class="y">2&times; USB 3.0+ Type-C (female), host mode &middot; output 5 V at 0.9 A maximum &middot; no USB Power Delivery [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] &mdash; this is what carries a room camera and mic for wireless BYOM. [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025] lists the port count only, without a generation or connector type, so the sheet is less specific than the requirement rather than in conflict with it. [Ruled by Damian Blazy 26 Aug 2026, superseding the 13 Aug 2026 ruling that called the connector USB-A: the hardware requirement has specified Type-C since Sept 2023]</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Compute: the silicon behind the workspace</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr><tr><td>Chipset / SoC</td><td>MediaTek MT8395 (Genio 1200): ARM SoC with Mali GPU and on-die APU, OpenGL ES 3.0 / Vulkan. The Gen 4 Pod ships as part of Mersive Pro, and the MT8395 was independently identified in the May 2025 device assessment.</td></tr><tr><td>NPU: on-device AI silicon</td><td>NPU requirement minimum 4.0 TOPS, goal 5.0 TOPS [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]; silicon capability 10+ TOPS [confirmed by Damian Blazy, 14 Aug 2026] &mdash; the SoC's APU. No inference runtime ships in the current image, so nothing on the device uses it yet [verify: the tflite DISTRO_FEATURE is commented out in mersive.inc:20-21 and is the only switch that pulls the runtime in. Confirm the roadmap for enabling it - engineering]</td></tr><tr><td>RAM &middot; storage</td><td class="y">Requirement: 16 GB LPDDR4x or better &middot; 32 GB eMMC or more [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]. As built: 16 GB LPDDR4 &middot; 32 GB flash &mdash; the capacities were observed directly in the May 2025 third-party device assessment, which identified Micron DDR4 parts and a 32 GB flash package [confirmed by Damian Blazy 13 Aug 2026: 16 GB LPDDR4 and 32 GB flash are proven]</td></tr><tr><td>Video pipeline: simultaneous decode / real-time encode</td><td>Decode 2&times; 4K60 or 4&times; 1080p60 concurrently [PRD workload target]; the compositor imposes no cap at the layout layer; the published Pro ceiling is 10 simultaneous shares, set by decode throughput</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Power &amp; physical</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:34%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Pro</th></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td class="y">PoE+ (802.3at Type 2) for single-cable install, or 12 VDC, 2 A, 24 W max from the barrel jack. <b>The power supply is sold separately</b> on Pro &mdash; it is in the box on Essentials, because a Pro room is usually powered over Ethernet and a second brick would be waste [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensions &middot; weight</td><td class="y">86 &times; 184 &times; 30 mm [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] &middot; [weight unverified - hardware team]. Pro and Essentials are <b>different chassis</b>: Essentials is 58 &times; 140 &times; 30 mm [Ruled by Damian Blazy 26 Aug 2026]. Both released data sheets print 150 &times; 67 &times; 30 mm and 204 g for both products, which cannot be true of two different enclosures; the sheets are being reissued [verify: mounting options and VESA/bracket accessories - product marketing]</td></tr><tr><td>Operating environment</td><td>Indoor use only &middot; 0&deg;C (32&deg;F) to 35&deg;C (95&deg;F) &middot; 30% to 70% RH ambient [Pro data sheet DS-MCS.PRO-05072025]</td></tr><tr><td>Storage environment</td><td>-20&deg;C (-4&deg;F) to 65&deg;C (149&deg;F) &middot; 10% to 90% RH [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr><tr><td>Regulatory</td><td>Required for launch: FCC (USA) &middot; UL (USA/Canada) &middot; CB Scheme / IECEE (international) &middot; UKCA (UK) &middot; CE (EU) &middot; RoHS (EU) &middot; SCIP (EU) [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] [verify: IC (Innovation Canada) was published here but appears in neither hardware requirement document - regulatory and legal] [verify: no TAA or NDAA statement appears on either released data sheet - legal and supply chain]</td></tr></table></div></details>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="upsell">
    <div class="g3band reveal"><b>Pro continues Mersive Solstice Gen 3.</b> Same lineage: your rooms, workflows, and muscle memory carry forward, and a mixed Gen 3 + Polaris estate is fully supported while you move. <a class="btn accent" href="/products/transition">The transition path →</a></div>
  </Fragment>
</ProductShowcase>
